@chapter 17 | Organic Chemistry, Polymers, Acids and Explosives

@intro
Organic chemistry in UPPSC is practical: **methane** from paddy fields, **ethylene** and **calcium carbide** for ripening fruit, methyl alcohol ("wood spirit") and beer, **polymers** natural and synthetic (rubber, silk, nylon, polythene, Teflon, Kevlar, Lexan, Bakelite, PVC), **organic acids** in food (lactic, acetic, tartaric, citric, formic, oxalic), and **explosives** (TNT, RDX, nitroglycerine). It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 17.1 | Basic Organic Compounds

@q 25:1-2

All organic compounds contain **carbon** (usually with hydrogen). **Methane** (CH_{4}), the simplest hydrocarbon, comes from waterlogged **paddy fields**, cattle (enteric fermentation), wetlands, landfills and coal mines; it is the main part of natural gas, biogas and CNG.

@q 25:3-5

**Ethylene** (C_{2}H_{4}) is the natural **ripening hormone** of fruits. **Ethephon** releases ethylene and is the approved ripening agent. **Calcium carbide** gives off **acetylene**, which also ripens fruit, but it carries arsenic and phosphorus impurities and is **banned** for this use by FSSAI.

@q 25:6-7

@table
Alcohol | Source / use
**Methyl alcohol (methanol)** | "**Wood spirit**" (from wood distillation); **poisonous**, causes blindness and death (spurious liquor)
**Ethyl alcohol (ethanol)** | From fermenting sugar (molasses) or grain; drinks, **ethanol blending** in petrol (E20), sanitiser
Ethylene glycol | Antifreeze
Glycerol | Soaps, cosmetics, **nitroglycerine**
**Beer** | Fermented **germinated barley** (malt) with hops
Wine | Fermented **grapes**
Rum | Molasses / sugarcane
Vodka | Potatoes or grain, distilled
@end

@section 17.2 | Polymers

@q 26:1-3

@src1 26:1
@one
Which one of the following substances is a natural polymer?
@ans Silk
@end

@q 26:6

@q 26:13

**Polymers** are giant molecules made of repeating units (monomers). **Natural polymers**: starch, cellulose (cotton, wood), **proteins** (wool, **silk**, leather), natural **rubber** (polyisoprene), DNA. Silk's protein **fibroin** contains **nitrogen** like all proteins. **Ghee** is a fat, not a polymer.

@q 26:4-5

@table
Polymer | Monomer / type | Use
**Polythene** | **Ethylene** | Bags, bottles (thermoplastic)
**PVC** | Vinyl chloride | Pipes, wires; **not biodegradable**
Polystyrene | Styrene | Thermocol, packaging
**Teflon** (PTFE) | Tetrafluoroethylene | **Non-stick** pans
**Neoprene** | Chloroprene | **Synthetic rubber** (elastomer), not a thermoplastic
**Nylon** | Polyamide | First synthetic fibre (1935)
**Dacron / terylene** | Polyester | Clothes
**Bakelite** | **Phenol + formaldehyde** | Switches, handles (**thermosetting**)
**Kevlar** | Aromatic **polyamide** | **Bullet-proof vests**, helmets
**Lexan** | **Polycarbonate** | **Bullet-proof windows**, CDs, visors
Glyptal | Alkyd resin | Paints, lacquers
Gutta-percha | Natural polyisoprene | Old cable insulation, dentistry
@end

**Thermoplastics** soften on heating and can be remoulded (polythene, PVC, polystyrene, Teflon); **thermosetting** plastics set permanently (Bakelite, melamine).

@q 26:7-11

@q 26:12

@q 26:14-15

**Glazing** gives pottery a glassy coat using silica and metal oxides such as zinc oxide or lead oxide. **Biodegradable** polymers (starch, cellulose, protein, PHBV) are broken down by microbes; most synthetic plastics such as **PVC** and polythene are not. India banned many **single-use plastic** items from 1 July 2022.

